# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repository stores LF, but this machine has core.autocrlf=true, so a Windows
# checkout rewrites text files to CRLF in the working tree. For most files that is
# harmless. For a shell script copied into a Linux container it is fatal, and the
# error tells you nothing useful:
#
#     exec /app/scripts/entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# The file is plainly there. What is missing is the interpreter — a CRLF shebang
# reads as "#!/bin/bash\r", so Linux looks for a program literally named
# "/bin/bash\r" and reports the SCRIPT as not found. That cost a full debugging
# round on 2026-09-18 when the HL7 test container crash-looped on startup.
#
# eol=lf pins the WORKING TREE, not just the index. The index was always correct;
# checkout was the step doing the damage, so normalising on commit would have fixed
# nothing.

# Let git decide what is text, and store LF for all of it.
* text=auto

# Consumed by Linux at runtime — must be LF on disk, on every platform.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf

# Systemd unit read inside the container/host Linux side.
*.service       text eol=lf

# Binary: never touch these, and never try to diff them as text.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.xlsx          binary
*.docx          binary
*.odt           binary
*.doc           binary
*.zip           binary
*.pem           binary
*.crt           binary
*.key           binary
